an is to danny rand what the  mcu's loki is to thor just as loki is  jealous of the love and admiration all  of asgard feels for the god of thunder  davos envies dany for winning the mantle  of iron fist even more davos hates dany  for what he sees as squandering that  power he's dany's primary antagonist in  season 2 of iron fist going to  increasingly darker lengths to claim  rand's power for his own since davos is  introduced in the first season we get to  watch his descent from danny rand's ally  to his nemesis  i'm not going to give up hope that one  day  you will come back to yourself  dejuan is believable as the villain  known in the comics as the steel serpent  and his conflict with rend is one of the  things that makes iron fist's second  season a vast improvement from the first  while the hand pops up in both daredevil  and iron fist it's finally in the  defenders that we meet its leader  alexandra reed sigourney weaver doesn't  play villains often enough and her time  as alexandra reid is proof weaver is  perfectly chosen as the hand's otherwise  immortal leader who finally feels the  approach of death as read she exudes  power ruthlessness and nothing more than  perhaps some dismissive pity for the  band of heroes trying to stop her sadly  while weaver is one of the best reasons  to watch the defenders that list isn't  very long among other flaws the series  was too short and packed with too many  characters before we hardly get a chance  to enjoy weaver in this villainous role  elektra kills her it's a shame that when  it's weaver's time to join the mcu it  was in a role that didn't have the time  to stretch its legs  sure the first season of iron fist is  probably one of the weakest seasons of  all of marvel's netflix series but as a  bad guy harold meacham is pretty great  the manipulative solitary killer spends  much of the series in his penthouse  plotting and abusing his grown children  then there's the brokenness he brought  to danny's family as he was the one who  arranged for the deaths of danny's  parents david wenham is great as meacham  who delves deeper into insanity as the  season continues caught between iron  fist and the hand one of is likely best  known as boromir's more resilient  brother pharamir in the lord of the  rings films so it's refreshingly  unsettling to see him in a role that  pushes so hard in the other direction  it's too bad the season as a whole  wasn't as good as wenham's performance  alyssa jones played by janet mcteer  never really had much of a chance in  jessica jones's second season we learned  she survived the car crash that killed  her son and husband after which she was  nursed back to health and given powers  by the scientist carl malus by the time  melissa and jessica are reunited  alyssa's dedication to malice reaches a  manic pitch making her dangerous to  anyone near her especially after malice  dies after the much more cerebral and  emotional threat of kilgrave and jessica  jones's first season someone who could  pose more of a physical challenge to the  hero was the obvious choice and mcteer  is wonderful in the role she brings a  fierceness to alyssa that makes you  believe she's capable of anything  including hurting or even killing the  daughter she's just been reunited with  the assassin john pilgrim played by josh  stewart is singular in the ranks of  punisher villains this is for no other  reason than the fact that the guy  actually survives his clash with frank  castle with his priest's collar and the  unsettling calm with which he doles out  violence pilgrim is one of the most  chilling and disturbing villains to show  up in the punisher or any of marvel's  netflix shows we eventually learn that  as skilled as he is at killing pilgrim  is far from being a conscienceless  killer a former member of the aryan  brotherhood pilgrim eventually saw the  evil of his ways converted to  christianity and became a pastor his  bloody rampage through the second season  of the punisher is the result of the  manipulative schultz's keeping his  children hostage it's pilgrim's  dedication to his family that convinces  frank to spare his life in spite of his  crimes  trish walker is perhaps the most tragic  character in any of marvel's netflix  series when we meet her in the first  season of jessica jones she is the  titular hero's best friend and the  survivor of a horrible assault as the  series continues her desire to be more  like jessica leads her to increasingly  dangerous extremes unfortunately this  includes her taking the same addicting  medications that gave the late will  simpson his enhanced abilities surprised  to find herself with powers walker goes  down roads she can't turn back from in  the show's final season she becomes a  self-styled vigilante but her time as a  crime fighter leaves a lot to be desired  among other things she accidentally  causes one attacker to fall on her own  weapon and flees the scene rather than  helping she kills gregory salinger just  before the series finale and the final  battle of jessica jones proves to be  between jones and walker  william rollins played by paul schultz  is precisely the kind of guy frank  castle was born to kill when castle  first meets rollins in afghanistan the  cia agent seems like someone castle is  destined to lock horns with this is even  before castle learns that rollins and  schooner are using their squad to  smuggle heroin back to the united states  it's also rollins who orders the hit on  castle's life leading to deaths of his  wife and their two children so  inevitably it is rollins who meets his  own brutal end at castle's hands before  the punisher's first season is over with  his freezing glare and permanent scowl  paul schultz is utterly believable as  the corrupt operative who left his  conscience far behind long ago he's the  perfect villain for the punisher's  inaugural season and his heir of cold  arrogance clashes wonderfully with the  more likable villainy of frank castle's  old friend billy russo  what do you say about a man who almost  kills a chef because he doesn't like how  he cooks a steak well you call him  whatever you want just make sure it's  out of his earshot in the final season  of jessica jones jeremy bob plays  gregory salinger a serial killer who  targets anyone he views as being morally  bankrupt or unworthy of their place in  society could be bad chefs blackmailers  or superpowered private detectives  salinger is highly intelligent and has  spent his life honing the different  skills he would need to be a prolific  serial killer but as challenging an  enemy as he is to jessica jones his  crushing self-loathing and self-doubt  shine through more than anything else  it's clear that no matter how much he  accomplishes or what he proves himself  capable of the voices inside salinger  will never allow it to be enough  and that  is extraordinary  in the third season of daredevil  benjamin poindexter played by wilson  bethel is an fbi agent who wilson fisk  manipulates into becoming his worst self  bethel plays a man who's learned to  manage his mental illness fisk learns  poindexter's coping skills and works to  tear them all down so that all  poindexter is left with is madness as a  killer poindexter is arguably the  greatest physical challenge murdock  faces in the course of his series  poindexter's backstory was an addition  not found in his comic character where  he goes by bullseye he is never directly  referred to as bullseye in the show  however the end of season 3 strongly  hinted that we were going to see the  return of the former fbi agent were the  series to have continued maybe he would  have come back as bullseye  guess we'll never know  few villains change as drastically  between seasons as the punishers billy  russo played by ben barnes in the  beginning of the first season of the  punisher russo acts like frank castle's  friend but we soon learned he was in bed  with rollins and schooner in the  cerberus squad of all of castle's  enemies from his special ops days russo  is the only one to survive the first  season but he doesn't do it unscathed  castle leaves russo with the scars that  earn him the name jigsaw traumatized by  his confrontation with castle russo  temporarily loses all memory of the last  few years in the punisher's second  season here actor ben barnes shows us a  more vulnerable side of jigsaw who  spends months recovering mentally and  physically ultimately billy russo acts  true to form seducing his therapist and  building a new gang of crooks and  killers but castle gives russo the death  he deserves in the end in his final  moments russo apologizes to his old  friend but castle hardly seems to care  it isn't tough to imagine how cornell  stokes aka cottonmouth won the loyalty  of the crooks under his command along  with the more obvious threats and  incentives someone like stokes would use  to build a criminal organization of all  the villains we meet in luke cage he has  the most magnetic personality mahershala  ali's brilliant performance is  mesmerizing enough that you actually  believe the confidence of that big laugh  of his it doesn't matter that one  confrontation after another shows he has  no good ideas about how to handle luke  cage his death at mariah dillard's hands  in the middle of the first season is  that much more shocking because of ali's  work even as mariah furiously finishes  him off with a mic stand it's tough to  believe the series is getting rid of one  of its most entertaining characters so  soon  bushmaster played by mustafa shakir is  the first villain in luke cage to give  the titular hero any kind of physical  challenge without the help of judas  bullets or power suits in the opening  moments of season two's i get physical  bushmaster leaves luke cage unconscious  on the harlem sidewalk using only his  martial arts skills his speed and his  enhanced strength the brooklyn native  uses the jamaican herb nightshade to  boost his strength and durability  rendering his skin almost as impervious  as cages bushmaster is a clever  charismatic bad guy and it's tough not  to sympathize with him he's in harlem  looking for vengeance on the stokes's  his own family was killed by the late  stokes matriarch mama mabel and her gang  cage and bushmaster eventually forge an  uneasy alliance to stop mariah dillard  but it ends when bushmaster proves more  willing to resort to murder than cage  would like  two episodes before wilson fisk appears  in daredevil's first season were  introduced to his most trusted advisor  james wesley played by toby leonard  moore well-dressed soft-spoken and  eloquent wesley is likely one of the  last men you'd guess is a high-ranking  member in a violent criminal  organization  yet in his first scene wesley calmly  sits on a park bench next to a man who  owes fisk money and delivers a monstrous  threat against the man's daughter i see  this man here  if we're being honest with each other mr  farnam i find his methods  unpleasant  it's because wesley isn't the guy who  works with guns or knives that he's such  a great villain he delivers his blows  with a word or a look wesley represents  the more civilized side of wilson fisk  the part of himself that the crime lord  wants the world to see that's a big part  of the reason why when karen page kills  wesley fisk begins to do away with his  photogenic facade and deal with his  enemies much more brazenly  while it wasn't enough to save the  series iron fist's second season is a  vast improvement on the first one of the  things that makes season 2 so much  better is alice eve's portrayal of the  mentally fractured mary walker every  scene she's in makes you worry about  what she might do employed by joy  meacham first to follow danny rand and  then to get more hands on walker is one  of the most intimidating and challenging  foes rand faces in the series in spite  of having no enhanced abilities through  skill and careful planning mary wipes  the floor with dany rand in their first  fight on a subway platform in target  iron fist  madame gao played by wai ching-ho is one  of the most dangerous people you'll meet  in marvel's netflix shows she presents  herself as a physically frail elderly  woman in order to give enemies and  allies alike a false sense of security  she prefers to work from the shadows  letting her actions speak for her and  foregoing the noise and bravado of other  bad guys while she tends to be a puppet  master more than anything else we learn  in the defenders that gal has physical  abilities that allow her to go  toe-to-toe with iron fist  not only is madame gao one of the  strongest pieces of connective tissue  throughout marvel's netflix series she's  perhaps the absolute last person you  want to mess with she may not lash out  in aggressive and bloody ways but she'll  give you that wicked smile and just wait  until the time is right  in the first half of luke cage's first  season mariah dillard wants harlem to  see her as the legitimate face of the  stove's family so she allows cornell to  lord over the criminal elements of the  neighborhood things change when he  implies the sexual abuse dillard endured  at the hands of her uncle was consensual  she kills cornell in his club and  supported by the manipulative shades  soon becomes the queen of crime in  harlem alfree woodard may have already  had a role in the mcu as a grieving  mother in 2016's captain america civil  war however her performance in luke cage  overrides any concerns a continuity  stickler might have through woodard  mariah is a tortured villain haunted by  the abuse she suffered from her criminal  family her rage and pain is bubbling at  the surface of every scene and she's  merciless enough to leave no mystery as  to how she could ever be a threat to a  man with bulletproof skin  few of marvel's netflix villains could  leave such a lasting impression as  jessica jones's kilgrave played by david  tennant gifted with the ability to make  anyone do what he tells them kilgrave  makes every character a potential time  bomb before the events of the series he  uses his powers to force jessica jones  to do a lot of things she doesn't want  to do including commit murder the trauma  she suffers at his hands is what makes  the hero so miserable and his presence  is felt throughout the show's amazing  first season even long before he and  jessica finally reunite face to face  david tennant's performance is such that  even though jones kills him in the  season 1 finale he never fully leaves  the series kilgrave leaves scars in  jessica too deep to let him die for good  we see this most clearly in season two's  aka three lives and counting when jones  is plagued by visions of kilgrave  harassing her and tempting her to lash  out with violence  it's unthinkable to imagine someone  other than vincent d'onofrio in the role  of daredevil's most important villain  wilson fisk for the show's three seasons  which is more than most of the marvel  netflix series got and yet hardly feels  like enough d'onofrio's time as fisk was  one of the best reasons to watch  daredevil d'onofrio provides the perfect  mix of intelligence civility  ruthlessness rage and turbulent pain to  bring us the man the comic books call  the kingpin it's probably not a  coincidence that season 2 is generally  regarded as the weakest chapter of  daredevil since fisk is mostly absent he  only appears in the final moments of the  season's eighth episode guilty as sin  wilson fisk embodies the incredible  potential of the stories that were being  told on netflix's marvel shows fisk is  no alien warlord or an asgardian god  jealous of his brother he's a man of  wealth whose childhood scars drive him  to evil and nothing about that sounds  like make-believe there's no reason why  someone like wilson fisk couldn't exist  in the real world and that makes him  more terrifying than any other mcu  villain you can think of  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite superheroes and villains  are coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

